WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.540
This episode is sponsored by Audible.

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:16.340
To get started, go to Audible.com/microcosmos
or text microcosmos to five hundred five hundred.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:25.179
There’s something you probably heard a lot
in biology class.

00:00:25.179 --> 00:00:31.380
And no, I do not mean, “mitochondria is
the powerhouse of the cell,” which, I mean,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.300
it is.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.320
Instead I’m talking about these two words:
“structure” and “function.”

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.500
In biology the way bits and pieces of an organism’s
body are built - from the materials to the

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:50.190
shapes to the arrangements—all these factors
that impact what an organism looks like, its

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:55.760
structure, are connected to what those bits
and pieces do.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.440
The function.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.210
Now this seems very simple, but the point
is that structure and function are linked.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:10.689
To observe one is to make predictions and
inferences about the other.

00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:16.539
And this is something we can see across all
levels of biology, from the shape of a bird’s

00:01:16.539 --> 00:01:21.789
wing to why mitochondria are the powerhouse
of the cell.

00:01:21.789 --> 00:01:27.380
And of course, structure and function relate
together in the ways that an organism eats.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:32.329
In animals, you might think of the different
types of teeth and mouths that reflect both

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:34.670
how and what a species eats.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:41.850
But this, of course, extends into the microcosmos
as well—not in terms of teeth, of course,

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.859
but in other structures that influence an
organism’s behavior and function as they

00:01:46.859 --> 00:01:48.990
pursue their meal of choice.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:55.459
And to see more of this, let’s focus on
one major group of eukaryotes that encompass

00:01:55.459 --> 00:02:00.130
a multitude of eating styles: the euglenoids.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:05.170
The euglenoids, or euglenids, are a large
group of flagellates made up of well over

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:06.719
1,000 species.

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:14.190
The primary trait uniting them are the strips
of proteins and microbes that line their bodies,

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:19.410
creating a visible and striking structure
called the pellicle.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:25.560
And some euglenoids are green—very, very
green, as we’ve explored before in a previous

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:26.790
episode.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:32.430
Their color comes from the chloroplasts inside
their body, which enable them to generate

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.930
their own nutrition via photosynthesis.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:39.360
But euglenoids weren’t always phototrophic.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:47.450
Now, we’re used to thinking of plants as
in some way less developed than animals.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:52.390
That things that make their energy from the
sun, are more primitive than the things that

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:55.780
get their energy by eating other stuff.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.020
This actually is often the reverse in the
microcosmos.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:07.960
As is the story for several eukaryotic lineages,
at some point a long, long time ago, a non-photosynthetic

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:11.070
euglenoid consumed a green algae.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:16.790
But instead of merely digesting that algae
and turning it into food, it formed an endosymbiotic

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:24.050
relationship that would eventually turn the
algae into a chloroplast and turn the phagotrophic

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:29.340
euglenoid, or one that survives by eating
stuff, into a phototroph.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:35.990
So, then the question becomes, what happened
to the organisms in those lineages that didn’t

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.800
make this dietary switch?

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:44.840
Well, in the case of euglenoids, they continued
evolving, just with a little less color.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.440
And by comparing these different euglenoids,
we can not only see how different forms of

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:57.120
eating connect across evolutionary webs, but
also how they become reflected in the structure

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.560
of the organisms themselves.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:08.290
Let’s start by explaining what the non-photosynthetic
euglenoids do for food.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:13.391
And you will notice here that I did not say
“What they eat” because they don’t all

00:04:13.391 --> 00:04:14.391
eat.

00:04:14.391 --> 00:04:18.570
We can divide these colorless euglenoids into
three groups.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:28.110
You’ve got your bacteriovores, your eukaryovores,
and your primary osmotrophs.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:34.480
Bacteriovores and eukaryovores are both phagotrophic,
meaning they rely on phagocytosis to engulf

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.200
and consume their food.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:41.290
Where they differ, of course, is what they
eat, which you can probably guess.

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:47.690
Bacteriovores, like this Dinematomonas, which
I have no idea if I’m pronouncing correctly,

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:54.940
usually go after smaller organisms like bacteria,
while eukaryovores like to super size their

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:59.780
meals and feed on larger microbial life like
eukaryotes.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:06.760
The last category of euglenoids, the primary
osmotrophs, are the ones who survive by absorbing

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.110
nutrients from their surroundings.

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:17.560
While phototrophs and phagotrophs are able
to get food in this as well, for primary osmotrophs,

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:21.590
this is it—food-wise, it’s absorption
or bust.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:34.340
So, they do not eat, they just allow stuff
to ooze into them.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:38.710
There is an evolutionary trajectory to these
categories.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:42.520
The bacteriovore euglenoids came first.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.520
Eukaryovores evolved from bacteriovores.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:51.400
And then phototrophs and primary osmotrophs
evolved independently from eukaryovores.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:57.290
There are, of course, a few exceptions to
this neat narrative, including a few osmotroph

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:00.430
species that evolved from phototrophic euglenoids.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:07.860
But for the most part, molecular phylogeny
has held up this order of dietary evolution.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:14.050
And what these organisms eat dictates how
much they interact with their environment,

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:18.960
which in turn requires different things out
of their bodies.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:25.460
Perhaps the most obvious divergence compared
to phototrophs is that these colorless euglenoids

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:26.460
are colorless.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:32.420
They don’t have the green chloroplasts that,
through their own chemistry and structure,

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:36.230
help phototrophic euglenoids convert sunlight
into food.

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:42.160
In the case of the phagotrophs then, they
need a way to grab onto the organism they

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:48.699
want to consume, and they do that with the
help of a feeding apparatus, which unfortunately

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:52.660
we can’t quite make out in our footage.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:59.340
This apparatus is made up of microtubules
that extend out from the front of the cell

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.590
and twist open to grab their prey.

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:09.090
As the organism retracts its feeding apparatus
back, it drags the food in with it.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.900
But osmotrophs and phototrophs don’t need
that apparatus.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.480
They’re not hunting anything down.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.401
And so it might not be much of a surprise
that these species tend to be marked by a

00:07:19.401 --> 00:07:23.139
reduction or even a loss of the feeding apparatus.

00:07:23.139 --> 00:07:29.600
Absorbtion or making your own nutrition also
requires a different sort of motility compared

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:32.100
to when you’re on the hunt.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:38.370
Osmotrophs and phototrophs tend to swim, which
allows them to explore and adjust to sunlight

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:41.580
and nutrition availability along a water column.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:47.360
Meanwhile, phagotrophs tend to glide along
surfaces, which enables them to search out

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.180
and find prey nearby.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:55.550
This behavioral difference has its basis,
of course, in structural differences.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:08:00.009
Most primary osmotrophs and phototrophs have
two flagella.

00:08:00.009 --> 00:08:05.729
But one is much more apparent and active than
the other, moving in a figure-eight shape

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:09.550
that helps the organism swim through the water.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:17.900
Meanwhile, phagotrophic flagella lie on different
ends of the cell and are lined with hairs

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:21.639
and rods that help the organism glide.

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:27.169
Their front flagellum extends straight out,
constantly moving to sense the world around

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:28.169
them.

00:08:28.169 --> 00:08:35.430
And for some species, their flagella is multipurpose:
they can use it to harpoon prey and move it

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:37.880
towards their feeding apparatus.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:45.760
But even though these phagotrophs may all
ingest via phagocytosis, not all food is the

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:51.649
same—differences that again are reflected
in the bodies of the hunters themselves.

00:08:51.649 --> 00:08:57.650
Eukaryovorous euglenoids tend to be larger
compared with their bacteria-consuming relatives,

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:04.149
and they also have more pellicular strips,
which allows them to be less rigid.

00:09:04.149 --> 00:09:10.560
Their size and stretch lets them not only
consume bigger food, but also to morph their

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.430
bodies according to what they digest.

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:18.760
The premise of “structure and function”
is one of those things that is so obvious

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:21.360
that it’s easy to dismiss sometimes.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.610
Of course the way a thing is built impacts
what it is able to do!

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:30.130
That’s the basis of how we make things that
are useful, whether they’re our homes or

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:31.640
our cars.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:37.270
But what makes this concept so fascinating
and powerful in biology is that these pieces

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:41.830
weren’t consciously assembled together—they
were evolved.

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:50.450
At one point, a bacteriovore euglenoid changed
in some way that gave way to a eukaryovore.

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:57.010
And eventually, some descendant of that eukaryovore
consumed a green algae that set the stage

00:09:57.010 --> 00:10:03.160
for phototrophs while another lost its feeding
apparatus to become an osmotroph.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:10.160
These differences in structure and function
link together a whole history of small changes

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:17.830
upon small changes, connecting organisms both
past and present through the much bigger differences

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:20.600
we observe today.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:26.670
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:30.050
And thank you as well to Audible, for supporting
